>> title: Lamb Tagine, Slow Cooker
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: lamb, moroccan, apricots, braise, slow cooker, crock pot
>> counters: The Slow Cooker
>> dish: lamb-tagine
>> kit: Slow Cooker
>> aka: lamb tajine, tajine, Moroccan lamb stew, slow cooker lamb tagine, crockpot tagine
>> pairs-with: harissa
>> servings: 6
>> time: 8 hr 40 min
>> slack: forgiving — lamb shoulder at eight hours and at ten is the same dish; the apricots are the only thing with a window and they go in at the end, when you are standing at the pot anyway
>> step.2: toss in the spice
>> step.3: brown 12 min in a skillet, in batches
>> step.4: soften the onions
>> step.5: braise on low, 8 hr
>> step.6: stir the apricots in, 20 min on high

Written for low, eight hours. A tagine is a slow vessel by definition, so this is a substitution a cook will forgive — an earthenware cone traded for an electric crock, both of them doing the same patient job. Swapping in a pressure cooker would be a different dish. The stock halves to one cup: a tagine's own conical lid returns its condensation to the pot, and so does this one.

Toss @lamb shoulder{3%lb}(1.4 kg; cut in 2-in pieces), @ground cumin{2%tsp}, @ground coriander{2%tsp}, @ground cinnamon{1%tsp}, @ground ginger{1%tsp}, @kosher salt{2%tsp} in a #bowl{}.

Brown @&(~1)spiced lamb{} in @olive oil{3%Tbs} in a #skillet{} for ~brown{12%min}, in batches — ground spice on a hot pan is thirty seconds from fragrant to burnt, so keep it moving.

Soften @yellow onions{2%large}(sliced), @garlic{4%cloves} in the same skillet with @&(~1)browned lamb{}, scraping everything into the pot.

Slow cook @&(~1)pot{} with @chicken stock{1%cup}(240 mL) in a #slow cooker{} on low for ~slow cook{8%hr}, lid on and left alone.

Stir @dried apricots{1%cup}(150 g), @honey{2%Tbs} into @&(~1)braised lamb{} and hold it on high, lid on, for ~slow cook{20%min} to plump them. In at the start they dissolve into the sauce and you lose the fruit entirely, which is most of what a tagine is for.
